  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nAs expected, the influence of thunderstorms and their outflow\nboundaries have largely kept higher heat indices away from a large \nportion of central Ohio. Otherwise, temperatures in the low to mid \n90s and dewpoints in the low 70s are supporting widespread heat \nindices of 100-105 degrees. \n\nThunderstorms overnight may largely impact the potential for another\nround of heat across southwest portions of the area on Saturday. \nGiven the uncertainty, will likely allow the current advisory to \nexpire at 8pm, then wait until the overnight activity occurs before \nissuing a new advisory. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nConditions have warmed up throughout the day and thunderstorms\ncontinue to develop and dissipate across the local area, struggling\nto organize to a level that would produce widespread damaging wind\nimpacts. There is plenty of instability and some wind shear exists,\nbut organization has been a challenge. This is primarily due to\nnorthwest to southeast boundaries not being favorable to the\nnorthwesterly steering flow. As the storms move farther southwest,\nthe wind shear is lower, limiting the potential for organized storms.\nThe orientation of the storms is much more favorable across southeast\nOhio into West Virginia, where a severe thunderstorm watch is\ncurrently in place. \n\nTo the northwest, another line of storms with a better directional \norientation is moving into northwest Ohio, however, recent trends \nhave shown a gradual weakening trend over the last hour or so.\nInstead, a focused area of convergence across east central Indiana\nand west-central Ohio could provide the best opportunity for more\nrobust thunderstorms through the afternoon. Damaging winds remain the\nprimary concern this afternoon and evening. \n\nIf a large thunderstorm complex fails to form and move through the\narea overnight, low-level jet driven thunderstorms may across\nthroughout the area into Saturday morning ahead of an approaching\nshortwave. This is typically found on the western edge of the\nthunderstorm activity from the previous day. As temperatures warm up\nSaturday morning, additional thunderstorms would then develop with\nthe renewed energy. Right now, the confidence in this occurring is\nover southwest portions of the area (southeast Indiana, southwest\nOhio, northern Kentucky). A damaging wind threat would once again\nexist with these thunderstorms. \n\nOverall, confidence remains low for any particular scenario. The\nridge breakdown will occur into the weekend, but the ultimate impacts\nacross the area are uncertain. \n\nWith PWATs well above normal, multiple thunderstorm complexes will\nprovide the opportunity for locally heavy rainfall and flash\nflooding, especially for areas where storms move over repeatedly.",
